  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday) \nIssued at 1145 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nWe continue to look for several features to come together late\nthis week to bring much needed rain into our area. The first will\nbe a disturbance with a pocket of tropical moisture coming up \nfrom the Gulf. The next will be a northern stream shortwave and\nsurface cold front. Finally, a big southern stream low pressure \nsystem will come from the west. This system will be pre-loaded \nwith Pacific tropical moisture. The interplay of these systems \nwill make for a complex setup determining exactly when and how \nmuch rain we get. \n\nLow to medium rain chances will enter the forecast Friday. This\nwill take the form of scattered showers and perhaps a few\nthunderstorms. If Nashville (BNA) goes the entire day Friday \nwithout measurable rainfall, that will officially break the record\nfor the most consecutive days without rainfall; 36 days is the \ncurrent record.\n\nBy Saturday, rain chances will increase area-wide as the various\nfeatures converge on our area, including arrival of the front\nfrom the northwest. Precipitable water values will reach over 2 \ninches, or 95th percentile climatologically, so a soaking rain\nevent is likely. Models have backed off on the number of ensemble\nmembers showing several inches of rain. Most likely we are \nlooking at one half to 1.5\" totals, but some areas could get \nheavier amounts depending on the development of any persistent \nbands of showers. The location and persistence of a main, heavier \nband of rain will depend a lot on the progress of the surface \nfront. Latest models are trending faster with the front, so it \nlooks like our southeast half has the best chance for higher \ntotals. Also with the front trending faster, the most significant,\nwidespread rainfall may get swept off to our southeast by mid \nmorning Sunday. Northwest areas could break out into partial \nsunshine for Sunday afternoon if the front scoots on through the \narea. But with an overrunning pattern, we may deal with post-\nfrontal low clouds and drip for a while, perhaps lasting into \nearly Monday. \n\nAfter the front completes passage through the area, we will be set\nup for a very nice pattern next week with dry air and comfortable\ntemperatures. This means a few clear days with lows mainly in the\n50s and highs in the 70s.",
